cuju reimplements Google Research Football (https://github.com/google-research/football),
Copyright 2019 Google LLC, Apache License 2.0. Its engine descends from Gameplay Football by
Bastiaan Konings Schuiling, dedicated to the public domain. Files that carry a Google line were
rewritten from that code by the cuju Authors in 2026. animations.bin and the asset bundles are
derived from the data and scenarios of Google Research Football.
cuju is not affiliated with or endorsed by Google.

include/cuju/libm ports math functions of the GNU C Library 2.39 (LGPL 2.1 or later). Where Arm
Optimized Routines v21.02 (MIT, Copyright Arm Limited) or FreeBSD's msun (the notice of Sun
Microsystems, Inc.) holds the same code, the port is taken from there under that license. Each file
reproduces its upstream notices and names its license. The libraries of cuju compile these
functions in, and the complete source of each release is published with it.

The renderer in python/cuju/render ships three.js (MIT, Copyright 2010-2026 three.js authors) and
Fabric.js (MIT, Copyright 2008-2015 Printio, Juriy Zaytsev and Maxim Chernyak), each with its
license beside it in web/vendor. Its player, ball, goal, kit and pitch assets are converted from the
media of Gameplay Football as Google Research Football ships it, dedicated to the public domain. The
player models in web/assets/models come from KayKit Character Packs Skeletons 1.1 and Adventurers
2.0, and the clips in web/assets/animations and the mannequin from KayKit Character Animations 1.1,
all by Kay Lousberg (www.kaylousberg.com), under Creative Commons Zero 1.0. The sounds in
web/assets/sounds are cut from Kenney's Sci-Fi Sounds 1.0 and Digital Audio (www.kenney.nl), from
recordings on Freesound, Wikimedia Commons, OpenGameArt and BigSoundBank dedicated under Creative
Commons Zero 1.0, and from works in the public domain by NASA, the National Park Service and
contributors to Wikimedia Commons. sounds.json beside them names the author, the source and the
license of each file.

The world RNG follows Boost.Random (Boost Software License 1.0). The episode seeds follow MT19937
by Makoto Matsumoto and Takuji Nishimura (BSD-3-Clause), as CPython's random module uses it.

The six training scenarios in the renderer are adapted from GRF_MARL by Light-MALib,
